{
  "id": "Compound::DB00219 - Gene::1128",
  "verbalization": "Oxyphenonium is likely to interact with Chrm1 through a series of molecular and cellular mechanisms. The evidence suggests that Oxyphenonium binds to Chrm3, which then participates in various receptor activities, including Acetylcholine Receptor Activity, G-protein Coupled Acetylcholine Receptor Activity, and Muscarinic Acetylcholine Receptors. These activities ultimately lead to the participation of Chrm1 in these processes. The integration of these pathways implies that Oxyphenonium exerts its effects on Chrm1 via a receptor-mediated mechanism, possibly through modulation of muscarinic or nicotinic receptors. The consistent binding to Chrm3 and the subsequent involvement of Chrm1 in receptor activities suggest a functional link between Oxyphenonium and Chrm1, mediated through the activation of cholinergic receptors. The strength of the evidence, while not definitive, supports a role for Oxyphenonium in influencing Chrm1 through cholinergic signaling pathways.",
